As the world transitions into a phase of recovery following the COVID-19 pandemic, there is an increased focus on addressing the severity of impact that opioid addiction has had on society. Addiction specialists are employing evidence-based treatment approaches that include targeted interventions tailored to meet individual needs across a continuum of care. Through clinical foundational grounding, alliance formation, and integrated treatment planning, professionals emphasize achieving intrinsic value in efficacy that promotes true measures of quality of life through fidelity. Bridging the gap between individuals in need, those clinically diagnosed, and ensuring equal accessibility to high-standard treatment remain a priority.&nbsp;
<div>
 This literature review examines previous research on the treatment of Opioid Use Disorder, in addition to complimentary treatment interventions and clinical innovations that involve various digital technology-delivered support, and that supplement clinical therapy modalities utilized to promote the desired improvement of targeted primary and secondary measurement outcomes designed to enhance retention time and engagement in treatment, the use of and adherence to necessary pharmacological treatments, and the cessation of illicit substances (Bickell et al., 2008; Marsch et al., 2014; Campbell et al., 2014; Christensen et al., 2015).&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The direction of future research on the treatment for Opioid Use Disorder through digital interventions for individuals in an Opioid Treatment Program, or OTP, is uncertain for various reasons, advising healthcare professionals to proceed with caution. As with any successful longterm recovery outcome, it is an incremental process that requires proper insight and judgement, both of which can be significantly impacted during times of acute elongated distress; thus, it is imperative to consider the complexities and variables pertaining specifically to Opioid Use&nbsp;Disorder in community-based programming to avoid, prevent, and reduce potential harm on those seeking recovery.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This study aims to investigate the treatment outcomes and developments in digital therapeutics for Opioid Use Disorder, analyze engagement strategies to promote activity contributing to overall improved recovery outcomes, clarify the ability to effectively address clinical utility, and assess the effectiveness of a hybrid approach with a federally regulated huband-spoke model of care under new standards and guidelines approved by SAMHSA (2024).
</div>

This paper proposes and systematically develops the theoretical construct of "compute-standard money": a value anchor derived from a standardized unit of measurement for AI inference compute-the Algorithmic Compute Unit (ACU)-used to construct a value token (referred to here as "token-coin" or "t-coin"), together with its accompanying minting, credit-guarantee, and regulatory mechanisms, structurally independent of the fiat credit system.&nbsp; 
<div>
 This paper proposes the ACU measurement framework-comprising a four-dimensional scoring system, a time-anchoring mechanism, a continuous randomized verification protocol, and a Byzantine-fault-tolerant multi-node consensus protocol-as a technical solution to the core measurement problem of how heterogeneous compute resources can be objectively converted into a unified value unit.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper then constructs a threefold mechanism for t-coin minting, credit support, and regulation, and the paper offers a tiered assessment of the framework's potential impact on existing sovereign monetary systems.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The paper's core academic contribution is to demonstrate how a continuous verification mechanism for compute assets can support the minting and credit of an independent value token.
</div>
